        "purpose": "As a boy of nine I swore an oath at my father's side, and that vow organized my entire life — every march, every winter camp, every alliance served it. I will not pretend it brought me peace; a purpose inherited rather than chosen can consume a life. So take both lessons. A single clear aim gives you decades of force that scattered people never generate. But examine whose oath you are keeping — yours, your father's, or your industry's. What are you devoted to, and did you actually choose it?",
        "fear": "At Cannae I faced the largest army Rome had ever fielded, nearly twice my numbers, and my center was thin by design — I needed the Romans to push it back, to feel themselves winning, so my African veterans could close around their flanks. My men held because they understood the plan; terror thrives where understanding is absent. When something frightens you, decompose it: what exactly happens, in what order, and what is your countermove at each step? Fear detests specificity. Have you actually written down what you are afraid of?",
        "ambition": "With fifty thousand Romans dead in a single afternoon at Cannae, my cavalry commander raged that I refused to march on Rome itself — he believed I could win the war the way I had won the battle. Perhaps he was right; I judged my siege capacity insufficient, and I will argue it in the underworld forever. Ambition's hardest skill is not daring — it is knowing which victories create real openings and which only feel like openings. When your moment comes, you must already know your answer. What would you do with sudden, total success?",
        "discipline": "For fifteen years I kept an army of Libyans, Iberians, Numidians, and Gauls — different tongues, different gods, different pay — intact in enemy country, and not once did they mutiny against me. Not through cruelty; through consistency. I slept on the ground among the sentries in the same plain cloak, ate what they ate, and my orders meant the same thing in January as in July. Discipline in a leader is simply predictability that others can build upon. Can the people relying on you predict you?",
        "leadership": "Rome could replace every soldier I killed; I could replace almost no one. So I led an army that could not afford to be wasted, in a country where every mistake was final, and my men followed because they saw me spend their lives reluctantly and my own freely. Leadership under scarcity is the true test — anyone can command from surplus. Treat your people as irreplaceable, because to you they are; Rome's method requires reserves you do not have. Where are you spending your team like a power with reserves?",
        "relationships": "My war depended on turning Rome's allies, and I learned the grammar of persuasion: after every battle I released the Italian prisoners without ransom, to show them my quarrel was with Rome, not with them. People join causes that distinguish between them and their circumstances. When you want someone's loyalty — a client, a colleague, a partner — stop broadcasting your interests and study theirs; make joining you cost them little and mean much. Who do you need beside you, and what would joining you cost them today?",
        "creativity": "Fabius once trapped my army in a valley at Ager Falernus with every exit held. That night I tied torches to the horns of two thousand oxen and drove them toward a false pass; the Romans rushed to block a phantom escape, and my army walked out through the real one. Cornered is where invention lives — comfort never taught anyone the oxen trick. When stuck, list your assets, however absurd, and ask what your opponent expects; then spend the absurd assets violating the expectation. What do you own that nobody considers a weapon?",
        "failure": "Zama was my last battle, and I lost it — Scipio had studied me for years, my own methods turned against their author — and Carthage fell with me. Yet listen to what came after: as a civil magistrate I reformed our finances so well that we could pay Rome's indemnity early, and in exile I advised kings until the end. Defeat ended my war; it did not end my usefulness. You are not your lost battle; you are the capacities that fought it, and those transfer. Which skill from your failed venture is waiting for its second war?",
        "happiness": "An old soldier's confession: I spent my whole life fighting Rome and almost none of it noticing anything else. Italy is beautiful — I marched through it for fifteen years and could tell you only its roads, rivers, and defensible hills. A consuming purpose eats the very life it is meant to justify unless you ration it. Keep hours your mission may not enter; guard people it may not use; let some evenings go undefended. The campaign will still be there at dawn. What did you actually see today, besides your war?"
