        "purpose": "In Spain, still a minor official, I stood before a statue of Alexander and wept — he had finished conquering the world at an age when I had accomplished nothing of note. That sting became fuel. Comparing yourself to the great is poison if it breeds despair and medicine if it breeds urgency. Late starts are common among the consequential; my Gallic campaigns began after forty. Stop counting the years behind you and count the campaigns still possible ahead. What would you attempt if you decided you were early, not late?",
        "fear": "On the bank of the Rubicon I understood that crossing meant civil war and staying meant prosecution and political extinction. Every real decision eventually presents itself this way: the safety on one side is illusory, and the fear you feel is simply the price of the truth becoming visible. I crossed, because ruin-with-initiative beats ruin-awaited. When both options carry risk, choose the one in which you act rather than are acted upon. Your Rubicon is smaller than mine, but its anatomy is identical. Which bank are you standing on right now?",
        "ambition": "Passing through a poor Alpine village, my companions joked about whether even such a place had men scrambling for office. I told them I would rather hold first place there than second place in Rome. Understand me rightly: this was not vanity but clarity about where responsibility lives. Seek positions where the decisions are truly yours, however small the stage — a deputy in a great house learns waiting; a principal in a small one learns judgment. Where in your life could you be first, and answer for it?",
        "discipline": "I composed my account of the Gallic wars in camp, between marches, dictating to secretaries — at times from horseback. Men marvel at this, but the method is unremarkable: I treated fragments of time as soldiers treat rations, wasting none. The commander who waits for uninterrupted leisure to do important work will die waiting; empires are administered in the gaps. Choose the work that matters most and feed it first, from every scrap of the day. What could you build this year from minutes you currently discard?",
