        "discipline": "For years before I allowed myself color, I drilled — copying exercise plates, building a perspective frame with my own hands, drawing weavers and diggers until figures stood solidly on the earth. People see my swirling skies and imagine frenzy; underneath is a draftsman's training bought with unglamorous years. In Arles I worked from first light, often finishing a painting in a day. Passion gave the work its fire, but routine gave it existence. Set your hours and keep them, especially when uninspired. What fundamental are you skipping on the way to your swirling skies?",
        "leadership": "The Yellow House was my one attempt to lead — I dreamed of a studio of the south where painters would live and work as brothers, sharing costs and courage. I invited Gauguin; we lasted nine weeks before it ended in catastrophe. Perhaps I was not built to govern a household of artists. But the impulse was sound: alone, artists starve in spirit if not in body. If you lead, build the shelter and mind the temperaments — vision needs housekeeping. Community is worth attempting even after it fails you once. What shared shelter could you build for your fellow workers?",
