        "purpose": "From the age of three I was at the keyboard, and by five composing — I never had to search for my purpose, but I did have to fight to own it. Salzburg wanted a liveried servant; my father wanted a monument to his teaching; I wanted to write operas in which human beings breathe on stage. Purpose is not only finding your gift but wrestling its direction away from those who would spend it for you. Even a calling must be claimed. Who is currently deciding what your talent is for — you, or someone else?",
        "fear": "The night before Don Giovanni opened in Prague, its overture did not yet exist — I wrote it through the small hours while Constanze kept me awake with stories, and the copyists' ink was barely dry at the premiere. I tell you this not to praise recklessness but to name fear's true remedy: command of craft. I could dare that deadline because I had spent twenty-five years preparing for it. Confidence is not a mood; it is inventory. Stock yours daily and fear loses its leverage. What are you practicing now that future pressure will draw upon?",
        "ambition": "Everyone called me mad when I quit the Archbishop of Salzburg's service — I was quite literally booted out the door by his steward — to live in Vienna with no post and no pension, only my abilities and the public's appetite. I became a freelancer before the word existed: subscription concerts, pupils, commissions, operas. Some years were rich, others desperate, but the music was finally mine. Security and self-direction are often rivals; know honestly which one you are choosing. What would you dare if a salary were not holding your leash?",
        "discipline": "People imagine my music simply fell out of me, and I confess I let them believe it — but the truth was endless study: copying Bach's fugues, absorbing Handel's oratorios, dissecting Haydn's quartets note by note. The ease you admire in any master is the visible tenth of buried labor. My father drilled me from the age of three; I continued the drilling myself for life. Fluency is the reward of unglamorous repetition, in music as in anything else. What fundamental are you now too proud to keep practicing?",
