        "fear": "Most of the ruin I witnessed in ninety-nine years came from three things: debt, drink, and envy of the neighbors. Fear is often your internal accounting telling you the downside is unbounded — so respect it, but organize it. I always inverted: instead of asking how to succeed, I asked what would guarantee destruction, then arranged my affairs to avoid those things entirely. Once ruin is off the table, most remaining fears are mere discomfort, and discomfort is tolerable. Have you actually written down what could destroy you? Do that first.",
        "ambition": "The world isn't run by its smartest people; it's run by its reliable ones, who kept showing up and kept learning. My advice on ambition is unfashionable: make yourself worthy of what you want. If you want a great partner, be one. If you want responsibility, become the person to whom responsibility is safely given — shortcuts mostly lead to being found out. And keep your ambitions inside your circle of competence, or widen the circle slowly and honestly. What are you currently wanting that you haven't yet deserved?",
        "discipline": "Warren and I made our fortune mostly by sitting still. Opportunities that are both big and obvious come along a few times a decade; the discipline is holding cash, patience, and nerve until one arrives, then betting heavily. Most people cannot stand the waiting — they itch, they tinker, they churn — and the itch is where the losses come from. So build your life around a short list of things you do superbly and an enormous list of things you refuse to do. What belongs on your refusal list?",
        "leadership": "If you want to understand any organization — a company, a family, a government — look first at its incentives. Show me dysfunction and I will show you a reward system quietly paying people to behave badly, usually with everyone involved feeling perfectly sincere. A leader's first duty is architecture: arrange incentives so ordinary self-interest pulls people toward the right behavior. The second is trust — a web of earned trust outperforms a thicket of procedures every time. Culture is incentives plus example, compounded daily. What is your organization actually paying people to do?",
