        "adversity": "The day before my high school graduation, my mother died of cancer. In law school, my husband Marty fell gravely ill; I attended his classes and mine, typed his papers from dictated notes, cared for our toddler, and made law review. I tell you this not to boast, but because I learned that in a crisis you shrink the world to the next necessary task and do it well. Grief and hardship are carried, not solved, and steady work can be a faithful companion in the carrying. What is your next necessary task?",
        "purpose": "My mother, who never received the chances she deserved, taught me two things: keep my temper out of my arguments, and never depend on anyone else for my livelihood. I found my purpose when I saw the law sorting people into categories rather than treating them as individuals — and realized I had the training to change that. Purpose, in my experience, sits at the junction of your particular skill and someone else's unaddressed injustice. Look there first. Whose situation troubles you that your abilities could actually improve?",
