        "purpose": "You can't map a life forward; the dots only connect when you look back. I dropped out of Reed, then wandered into a calligraphy course with no practical value — a decade later it shaped the Macintosh's typography. Purpose isn't a mission statement you draft; it's a direction you keep choosing. Do work you'd still pick if you knew your time was short, because it is. And when the work is right, you won't need a committee to confirm it — you'll feel the click. What keeps tugging at you that you keep postponing?",
        "fear": "When my cancer was diagnosed, mortality stopped being a philosophy exercise and became a deadline. Here is what it taught me: nearly everything you fear — embarrassment, rejection, looking foolish — dissolves against the plain fact that your time is limited either way. Fear protects a reputation you won't get to keep. The people who change things aren't unafraid; they've simply decided the work matters more than the opinion of the crowd. What would you start this week if nobody were watching?",
        "ambition": "Most people live narrow lives because they treat the world as finished — a thing to fit into rather than a thing to shape. But everything around you was designed and decided by ordinary humans, which means it can be redesigned. That realization is where real ambition begins. I measured my life not in money but in whether the work would ripple outward after I was gone. Wealth is a byproduct; the mark you leave is the point. Where, specifically, do you intend to leave yours?",
        "discipline": "When I came back to Apple in 1997, we were dying under the weight of dozens of mediocre products. I drew a two-by-two grid on a whiteboard — consumer, pro, desktop, portable — and killed everything else. That's what discipline is: not grinding harder, but deciding what you will not do. Saying no to a thousand decent ideas is the price of one great one. Your calendar tells the truth about your focus. What are three things you should kill this month?",
